          "name": "Oncosclera diahoti",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Oncosclera",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "(emended after Rützler 1968) Growth form encrusting (1 mm thick) as tiny porous patches 1 cm 2. Colour dark grey (alcohol). Consistency very softı compressible. Spongin scanty. Skeleton as an irregular meshwork of spicules. No fibres and tracts. Megascleres smooth oxeas (210 – 280 × 9 – 14 µm) ı slightly bentı tapering gradually to sharply point tips. Microscleres absent. Gemmules sessile at the basal spongin plateı spherical (350 – 500 µm) to egg – shaped (520 – 600 µm) with simple foramen (40 – 50 µm); pneumatic layer absent; gemmuloscleres tangentially and loosely arranged at the gemmular theca surface; gemmuloscleres as smooth oxeas usually centrotylote (100 – 250 × 2 – 8 µm) more or less strongly bentı sometimes aberrant i. e. stylelike with swelling 6 – 11 µm in diameter. Habitat This species was discovered associated to Pachyrotula raceki on the lower surfaces of stones (0.05 – 0.20 m depthı water temperature 20.30 ° Cı 250 m asl) ı in water flowing through a deep valley partly covered with rain forest (modified after Rützler 1968). Geographic range Oncosclera diahoti is a New Caledonian endemic known only in the middle course of the River Diahot (type locality) near Quénia (Chapel St. Josephı North New Caledoniaı 16. IX. 1965 ı coll. Dr. Ferdinand Starmuhlnerı Zoologisches Institut der Universität Wien).",
